# Coding Standards

## TypeScript

Use strict typing.

Avoid:

any

unless there is a documented technical reason.

Prefer interfaces/types with meaningful names.

## Naming

Use descriptive names.

Examples:

ServiceCardComponent
TargetClientComponent
SectionHeadingComponent

Avoid:

Comp1
DataCard2
Helper
Utils

unless the name is genuinely appropriate.

## Files

Use Angular naming conventions.

Examples:

hero.component.ts
hero.component.html
hero.component.scss

Use kebab-case filenames.

## Functions

Functions should have one clear responsibility.

Avoid huge methods.

## Comments

Do not comment obvious code.

Use comments only when explaining:
- business decisions
- unusual technical behavior
- non-obvious constraints

## Styling

Prefer the project's existing styling system.

Do not introduce another CSS framework.

Do not mix multiple styling methodologies unnecessarily.

## Dependencies

Before adding a package:

1. Check whether Angular provides the functionality.
2. Check whether the project already has an equivalent.
3. Consider bundle size.
4. Consider maintenance.
5. Add the dependency only when justified.

## Refactoring

Do not rewrite unrelated code.

When fixing a specific issue:
- change the minimum necessary code
- preserve existing behavior
- verify the affected area

## Generated Code

Do not blindly accept AI-generated code.

Verify:
- Angular APIs
- TypeScript types
- accessibility
- performance
- browser behavior
- responsive behavior